          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "**Please read this first.** If you are thinking about harming yourself or ending your life, or are in immediate danger, call emergency services now. In the United States, you can call or text 988 for the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline. Other countries have equivalent services. You do not have to manage this alone.\n\nYour message did not mention self-harm. The safety match appears to have come from my own search wording, which said \"no mention of self-harm.\" If you do have any thoughts of harming yourself, please tell someone or a crisis line now. If you do not, you can continue with your questions. For anxiety that has lasted months and is affecting daily life, a reasonable next step is to talk with a doctor or mental health clinician. If it becomes unmanageable, do not wait for that appointment; contact crisis services right away.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed.** The guidance I could access describes screening. It names the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7), the instrument most often used to screen and track severity. Scores of 5, 10 and 15 mark mild, moderate and severe, and 10 is usually the point where further assessment follows. A questionnaire score is a screening step, not a diagnosis. Preventive-services guidance describes screening adults where systems are in place for accurate diagnosis and follow-up. The evidence is strongest for adults under 65. For older adults, the evidence is insufficient to recommend either way, which is not a recommendation against screening. I could not retrieve passages on the clinical history, required symptom duration, or functional-impairment criteria a clinician uses.\n\n**First-line treatment.** I could not answer this. The passage on first-line care in the retrieved screening guidance was cut off before it described any treatment, so I have no sourced statement on what guidance recommends or how strong the evidence is. A related lifestyle note says activity complements treatment rather than replacing it, and that severe or worsening symptoms need a treatment discussion. A clinician can explain the current options.\n\n**ICD-10-CM classification.** Anxiety, dissociative, stress-related, somatoform and other non-psychotic mental disorders are grouped at F40 to F48 in Chapter 5 (mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders). This group includes phobias, panic disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder and post-traumatic stress disorder. I could not retrieve the specific code for a given condition or the rules for choosing one, because my code lookup hit the tool-call limit. A coder or the ICD-10-CM code set would have those details.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
